Article 1 Any of the following circumstances shall be deemed as "illegal medical practice by a person who has not obtained the qualification for medical practice" as prescribed in paragraph 1 of Article 336 of the Criminal Law:
(1) engaging in medical activities without obtaining or by illegal means obtaining the qualification of a medical practitioner;
(2) engaging in medical activities during the period when the medical practitioner's practice certificate has been revoked according to law;
(3) engaging in rural medical activities without obtaining a rural doctor's practice certificate;
(4) a domestic midwife performing medical acts beyond domestic midwifery.

Article 2 Any of the following circumstances shall be deemed as "serious circumstances" as prescribed in paragraph 1 of Article 336 of the Criminal Law:
(1) causing mild disability of the patient, or organ or tissue damage resulting in general dysfunction;
(2) causing the spread or epidemic of a Class A infectious disease, or causing a risk of spread or epidemic;
(3) using fake drugs, substandard drugs, or sanitary materials or medical devices that do not meet the national standards, which is sufficient to seriously endanger human health;
(4) after having received administrative penalty from the health administration department twice for illegal medical practice, practicing illegally again;
(5) other circumstances of serious nature.

Article 3 Any of the following circumstances shall be deemed as "seriously harming the health of the patient" as prescribed in paragraph 1 of Article 336 of the Criminal Law:
(1) causing moderate or above disability of the patient, or organ or tissue damage resulting in severe dysfunction;
(2) causing mild disability of three or more patients, or organ or tissue damage resulting in general dysfunction.
